<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Republican Party (United States)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Grand Old Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Republican Party is one of the two major political parties of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, founded in 1854 by opponents of the expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> into the western territories. Its first successful presidential candidate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>, was elected in 1860, and it dominated national politics for much of the period until 1932; it is often called the Grand Old Party (GOP).</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Republican Party began in 1854 as an anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> party, formed to oppose the expansion of slavery into the western territories after the Democrats under Stephan Douglas passed the Kansas-Nebraska Act. It was formed in the Northern and Border states but not in the South, and it rapidly gained support in the North, drawing in former Whigs, Free Soil Democrats and former Know Nothings. It emerged as the main rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democratic Party</a>, and the two have dominated American politics since.</p><p>The founders chose the name in homage to the republican values of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republican-party">Democratic-Republican Party</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> had called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republican-party-brazil">Republican Party</a>. The idea came from an editorial by the party's leading publicist, Horace Greeley, who asked for a simple name like Republican to designate those who had united to restore the Union to its true mission as champion of liberty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>'s election as president in 1860 led to the secession of Southern states and the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. Under Lincoln and a Republican-controlled Congress the party led the effort to preserve the Union, defeat the Confederacy and abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. By 1865 it encompassed northern Protestants, factory workers, professionals, businessmen, prosperous farmers and black former slaves. Radical Republicans, hard-line abolitionists, pressed for abolition as a war aim and clashed with Lincoln's moderate Reconstruction plans. During Reconstruction the party sought to extend civil rights protections to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a>, and by the late 1870s it turned its attention to business interests and industrial expansion.</p><p>In the late 19th and early 20th centuries it dominated national politics, promoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-tariff">protective tariffs</a> and infrastructure development. In the 20th century it split between an Old Right and a moderate faction in the Northeast, and opposition to Roosevelt's New Deal produced the conservative coalition. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>, the Southern states became more reliably Republican in presidential elections. The party won five of the six presidential elections from 1968 to 1988, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a>, in office from 1981 to 1989, was a transformative leader.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The nickname Grand Old Party originated in 1875 in the Congressional Record, which referred to the party of the Union's military defence as this gallant old party. The following year the Cincinnati Commercial used the phrase grand old party, and the abbreviation GOP is first recorded in 1884. Nineteen Republicans have served as president, the most from any single party.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Republican Party (United States)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Party politics emerged early, with Federalists against Jeffersonian Republicans, later Democrats against Whigs and, from the 1850s, Democrats against the new <b>Republican Party</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Republican Party</b> was organised that summer and won the presidency in 1860.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-20">20 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
